    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d314">

                <pb facs="0482" n="402" xml:id="pg402"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">762.94/99: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Poland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Warsaw</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-11-27T18:00:00+01:00">November 27,
                            1936—6 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 27—3:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>137. Reliably informed Poland has not slightest intention of joining
                    German-Japanese anti-Communist front. Colonel Beck<anchor n="15"
                        xml:id="d314-f1"/> remarked to me on 24th that “The Polish Government’s
                    relations with the Soviet Government are always formally correct.” Poland
                    ignores the Third International and paradoxically has less fear of Communist
                    propaganda than have many countries whose frontiers are not contiguous to
                    Russia.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d314-f1" n="15" xml:id="d314-f1-content">
                <p>Polish Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
